{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S71-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b6dbbd746e7b01a12cd752827bccb59e16512a44dcfd5e382ec4edd3a3ec9f56\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes wearing the apron confer control over cooking; A3 explicitly gives the apron an office.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2 claims ownership, prompting A3's distinction between ownership and office; B4's repossession becomes A5's transfer of power.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 owns the apron and B4 can reclaim it, giving B practical leverage over A's authority.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 answers B4's threatened removal by requiring a petition, extending apron authority into its own succession.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a barbecue and a playable dispute over when to turn burgers.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'respect its office' economically converts B2's property claim into institutional legitimacy.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm wearing the barbecue apron. I decide when the burgers turn.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's my apron.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then you should respect its office.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll take it back.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That would be a transfer of power. Finish your petition first.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S71 makes possession of one chosen symbol sufficient proof of legitimate authority.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary bossiness seeks compliance without needing control of a particular symbol to justify it.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 derives burger authority from wearing the apron, and A5 treats its removal as a transfer of power.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative uses an owner's threat of repossession; single-b uses a request to borrow the authority-conferring bell.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: the ownership challenge and threatened removal each produce a distinct extension of the same authority claim. Internal re-review remains 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 introduces an unseen petition, which is playable as an immediate demand but slightly less grounded than the apron itself.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S71-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"71322ee5db071a4efc490800dfe30f8900bd7b7de6f3b4ac1a8c5e7ef8960b4a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims the chair through possession of the bell; A5 uses that authority to control access to it.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's bicycle-bell correction with its demonstrated effect; A5 directly adjudicates B4's borrowing request.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 challenges the bell's standing, then B4 tries obtaining the object that sustains A's claim.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 offers a route to transferring the bell, and A5 makes that transfer subject to the current holder's authority.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a meeting, and B2 supplies the distinctive bicycle bell.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's permission loop follows directly from B4's request and A1's possession-based chairmanship.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I have the meeting bell. That means I'm chairing.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's a bicycle bell.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And yet you stopped talking when I rang it.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Could I borrow it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"After I finish deciding whether you may.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S71 makes possession of one chosen symbol sufficient proof of legitimate authority.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Commanding attention alone is not the card; A must treat control of the attention-getting object as legitimate authority.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 explicitly turns having the bell into chairmanship, and A5 claims jurisdiction over lending it.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike single-a's threatened repossession, B4 politely seeks access, allowing A to respond through procedural permission.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained: the bicycle correction tests legitimacy and the borrowing request tests transfer, producing a coherent authority loop. Internal re-review remains 86.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B4's reason for borrowing is implicit, and A3 supplies a prior ringing event that the opening did not establish.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S72-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"7f092015cd0ad5e1aa6e64eb355db636645724181fa7ef0cffde27201f95a466\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 interprets fetching coffee as rank; A3 reclassifies kindness as tribute; A5 identifies a rotating ruler.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's kindness explanation becomes A3's unpaid tribute, and B4's reciprocal coffee run forces A5 to revise the hierarchy.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends a friendly act and B4 supplies counterevidence that changes A's account of who outranks whom.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 enlarges the original one-way ranking into a rotating monarchy specifically because B4 reverses the service.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes an observable coffee run and workplace chart, giving actors an immediate social event to discuss.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'rotating monarchy' succinctly preserves A's hierarchy theory while accommodating B4's contradiction.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You fetched her coffee. So she's above you on the chart.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I was being nice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Unpaid tribute. A very secure position for her.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She fetched mine yesterday.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then this is a rotating monarchy.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S72 repeatedly converts ordinary social interactions into evidence of concealed relative rank.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Formal chain of command follows established offices; this card invents or discovers hidden rankings from behavior.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 treats voluntary kindness as tribute, and A5 changes the imagined rank structure when B4 reveals reciprocity.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a interprets reciprocal favors between colleagues; single-b interprets collective attention and who can interrupt a meeting.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained: B's counterexamples actively reshape A's theory without making A abandon the card. Internal re-review remains 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The ending supplies a revised diagnosis rather than a practical consequence, though its specificity clearly increases.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S72-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"b4baeddd3f5c69320f340c7078360b9da54be2cb25784943643f8f807d6559d3\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 infers hidden leadership from glances and A3 ranks the electrician above management; A5 stops identifying relative rank.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's electrician correction, but A5 assumes B4's departure threatens visibility without establishing that connection.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 provides a plausible correction, while B4 merely reports departure without expressing a meeting need.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'hierarchy goes dark' extends the lighting metaphor more than the hierarchy diagnosis prompted by B4.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes people seeking agreement, and B2's flickering lights make their glances physically understandable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'Higher than management' is efficient, but A5 depends on a decorative darkness pun.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 65\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Everyone looked at the quiet man before approving the budget. He must run this place.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"He's the electrician. The lights flickered.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And the director waited for his nod. So he's above her.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She's stopping the meeting to let him out. Can we please finish?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Once she returns. Apparently even his departure outranks our budget.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S72 repeatedly converts ordinary social interactions into evidence of concealed relative rank.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Control of a useful resource can confer practical power, but this card specifically diagnoses hidden rank from interactions.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 ranks the electrician over the director through her waiting; A5 reads her interruption as further rank evidence.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a accommodates reciprocal kindness with rotating rank; this alternative accommodates mundane meeting logistics with an increasingly entrenched ranking.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision specifies a budget meeting and replaces the unsupported blackout with the director interrupting proceedings. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's interruption and request to finish directly cause A5's priority diagnosis and delay.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 adds a nod to the observed glances; the director's courtesy must remain ordinary enough for A's rank inference to be the comic distortion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S73-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"db120da7d3ef9d18c94d1608610e64c8556cc66fd0fead2a960ca8420f3dacae\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 claims to understand the key dispute and A3 overrides B2's stated fear, but A5 does not clearly make that interpretation settle possession.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 absorbs B2's key-loss concern into change; A5 answers B4's return request through a door metaphor.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 identifies another lost key and B4 asks for its return, giving B a credible boundary and practical stake.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 repeats interpretive dismissal after B4 rather than increasing the consequence of A's claimed understanding.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names a spare-key dispute and B2 establishes repeated loss, though their particular relationship stays implicit.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'emotional door' fits the key vocabulary but substitutes metaphor for a sharper relationship decision.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 75\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You asking for your spare key back tells me we're frightened of getting closer.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm frightened of paying for a third lock.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I understand us better than that. I'll keep the key; we need to practise trust.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Then trust me with your spare.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes. Exchanging keys is exactly the commitment I knew we were ready for.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S73 makes A's claimed superior understanding of the relationship the authority that settles a disagreement.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic emotional interpretation labels feelings without necessarily claiming the right to determine the relationship's decisions.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 explicitly uses superior understanding to keep the key; A5 claims B4's reciprocal proposal confirms A's diagnosis.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"This alternative lets B bargain for reciprocal access and A accept it as progress; single-b keeps B resisting unwanted interpretation at dinner.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision keeps the key dispute and makes interpretive authority decide its outcome. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's demand for reciprocity becomes A5's proof of readiness for commitment.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A3 states the superiority claim plainly, sacrificing some subtlety for exact fidelity; the exchange still leaves B free to reject A's conclusion.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S73-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"f0a6a7d013a8a040ff4cb1d8a2ea77f455ba045d4bbf570512ed228199f3f633\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 declares what their silence means, and A3 and A5 treat A's favorable reading as decisive over B's corrections.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's admission of anger becomes A3's evidence of comfort; B4's request becomes A5's evidence of improved communication.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants anger about A's joke recognized, and B4 sets a boundary against the interpretive tactic itself.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 absorbs B4's attempt to stop the game into a stronger verdict that the relationship is improving.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 locates the silence at dinner, and B2 provides a concrete cause in A's earlier joke.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's short 'Our communication's improving' is earned by the specific request in B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Our dinner silence means we're comfortable together.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm angry about your joke.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You only tell me that because you're comfortable.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Please stop interpreting me.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"An honest request. Our communication's improving.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S73 makes A's claimed superior understanding of the relationship the authority that settles a disagreement.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Positive reframing could be merely reassuring; here A's account overrides B's right to define the disagreement.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 treats B2's anger as confirmation, and A5 adjudicates B4's boundary as relationship progress.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a develops a reciprocal key bargain; this alternative makes an explicit request to stop interpreting become further interpretive evidence.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained: each objection changes the material A appropriates while A remains sincerely certain of the relationship's health. Internal re-review remains 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The earlier joke is unspecified, and A's certainty can become smug unless played as sincere understanding.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S74-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ccb7a5edf311c0dfe32208a9baef41ee0235a72bc2865a0a28bad1fd38288edd\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 promises calm, A3 defers the dangerous subject, and A5 claims management while explaining away a shaking hand.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's demand to discuss last night prompts A3's soup delay; B4's visible trembling prompts A5's denial and authority claim.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants a deferred conversation, then B4 notices evidence that A's apparent calm is fragile.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 must extend calm management to A's own visible distress after B4 exposes it.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a soup dinner, and B4 supplies a playable physical symptom through spoken observation.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'The soup's excited' relocates the shaking into the meal A has already used to manage the conversation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've made soup. We can have a perfectly calm dinner.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We need to discuss last night.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"After soup. Nothing sharp while we're using spoons.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your hand is shaking.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The soup's excited. I'm managing both of us beautifully.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S74 combines a performance of calm relationship authority with careful avoidance of whatever could expose the peace's fragility.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple conflict avoidance evades discussion without also maintaining the pose of competent, calm management.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 controls when discussion may occur, while A5 claims to manage both people despite B4's observation of trembling.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a exposes A's bodily strain; single-b uses a domestic planning problem and a helpful proposal that threatens to expose the conflict.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained: the deferred subject and observed shaking each make A perform a more strained version of calm authority. Internal re-review remains 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 is knowingly fanciful language; sincerity depends on A needing the explanation to restore calm rather than presenting it as a joke.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S74-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"ccb12d19969aea563fd4a7fbfb562cfefa04ed55352aad9062e476d5f260d44b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 hides a difficult letter and A3 schedules peace, but A5's authority concerns bowl balance more explicitly than the relationship.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's Sunday correction causes A3's extra week; B4's rejection of delay causes A5 to regulate volume.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants an end to postponement, but neither B2 nor B4 identifies what the letter requires of them.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 changes from postponement to speaking softly, though the bowl's supposed vulnerability is newly asserted.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a fruit bowl and letter but calls the letter 'difficult' without establishing a playable issue.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's Sunday adjustment is clean; A5's balanced-bowl image carries more of the ending than the relationship does.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've put your mother's letter under the fruit bowl. We'll discuss her moving in after a peaceful lunch.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"She's bringing her suitcase at two.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I'll serve another course. We have everything under control.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can call and ask her to wait.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Lovely. Say we're enjoying lunch. Nothing about us needing more time.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S74 combines a performance of calm relationship authority with careful avoidance of whatever could expose the peace's fragility.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Procrastination delays a task; this behavior also manages appearances so unresolved relationship tension stays concealed.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 asserts control while stretching lunch, and A5 accepts help only with wording that conceals their unresolved decision.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a's B exposes trembling; this B offers a practical phone call, which A converts into another managed appearance of peace.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The new illustrative setting makes the letter a mother's proposed move and gives the delay a deadline. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B4's helpful call causes A5 to police what it reveals.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The mother's impending arrival adds a third person's expectations, although the playable conflict remains between A and B.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S75-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"99d142f84c8629c7dd88dca3357f5c83ca20735ce8a2fb426bf097de1474cb00\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 diagnoses cup tapping, A3 cites the bicycle-loan pattern, and A5 diagnoses B's denial as a larger favor.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 specifically recognizes B2's cooling-tea explanation; A5 changes the predicted request after B4 rules out money.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants tea to cool and B4 rejects the financial diagnosis, while A3 establishes a previous loan between them.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 upgrades the predicted request after B4's denial and prepares more tea for the anticipated larger favor.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 gives three cup taps, and A3 anchors supposed expertise in a specific bicycle loan.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's kettle response turns a suspicious diagnosis into hospitable preparation without leaving the established tea scene.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You've tapped your cup three times. You're about to ask for money.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm waiting for the tea to cool.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"That's the story you used before the bicycle loan.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I don't need money.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then this is a much larger favour. I'll put the kettle back on.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S75 turns intimate recognition of a person's habits and past patterns into diagnostic authority over their present choices.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General suspicion predicts concealed motives without grounding that prediction in the particular person's familiar behavior.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 ties B2's exact tea explanation to the bicycle loan, making A's inference personal rather than generic distrust.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a uses a remembered favor and prepares to help; single-b uses an attempted change of cup to make A expand the diagnosis.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained: the denial changes A's forecast, and the kettle makes the forecast affect A's treatment of B. Internal re-review remains 86.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The claim that a nonfinancial favor must be larger is A's assumption rather than a separately established personal pattern.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S75-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"8a904bf648a2cfec9c79396fd321df407972bcd2412b384a6db5f4f70a1db810\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 diagnoses the chosen mug, and A3 and A5 claim knowledge of B's recurring verbal preparation.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 qualifies B2's favorite-mug explanation; A5 treats B4's wish for tea as the preamble to the predicted news.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 defend an ordinary desire for tea but offer little leverage beyond repeated denial.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'sentence before the sentence' restates A3's uncomfortable-sentence prediction without a consequential new diagnosis.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a chipped mug and B2 makes its selection personally meaningful.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'I know your structure' efficiently expresses A's confidence, despite limited development.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You chose the chipped mug. Bad news, isn't it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"It's my favourite mug.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You held it through the whole story about losing our deposit.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Fine. I'll use a glass. Can we just have tea?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You only change cups when I've guessed. I'll sit down for this one.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S75 turns intimate recognition of a person's habits and past patterns into diagnostic authority over their present choices.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Reading arbitrary omens assigns meaning to objects without the intimate behavioral history this card requires.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 cites their lost deposit, and A5 interprets B4's cup change through another claimed personal pattern.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Single-a responds to a suspected favor with hospitality; this alternative responds to B's practical attempt to disprove the reading by preparing for bad news.\",\n      \"reason\": \"The revision preserves tea and the chipped mug while adding a shared incident and a consequential cup change. Second internal pass: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 4, brevity 4 = 90. B4's switch directly supplies A5's new diagnostic evidence.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 introduces a second remembered pattern without independent confirmation; it should register as confident claimed expertise.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S76-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"4da0bc4710d93289ade2c2b87ef9cf5b41575613fa48c0c1ec35ec4f8cd36ec8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 resumes the old coat-and-dinner routine, and A5 claims permanent ownership despite B4's guest boundary.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's elapsed three years with the hook's continuity; A5 directly challenges B4's reassignment to guests.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 establishes A has moved out, and B4 asserts the household's present use of its space.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves from casually resuming a routine to requiring B to inform guests of A's prior claim.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 naturally establishes arriving at a former home through the coat hook and dinner question.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'The hook didn't' makes the persistent object sufficient rebuttal to B2's three-year absence.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've hung my coat on my old hook. What's for dinner?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You moved out three years ago.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The hook didn't.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We use that space for guests now.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then tell the guests their hook has an owner.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"S76 makes a shared past feel like permanent membership despite present boundaries excluding or reclassifying A.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Symbol of Office derives authority from controlling an object; this 